\paragraph{SCS novelty.} SCS shares its formal definition (pairwise cosine similarity of type name embeddings) with the partitioning criterion used in LLM-PEE~\cite{llm-pee-2025} for schema decomposition. The use of embedding distance for type discrimination has parallels in metric learning \citep{snell2017prototypical} and zero-shot text classification via label embeddings \citep{yin-2019-zero-shot-bench}. Our contribution is not the metric itself but the controlled experimental framework that isolates confusability's predictive relationship with extraction degradation, distinguishing it from the engineering use in prior work. The modest pairwise effect size ($r^2 = 1.3$--$1.7\%$) is consistent with standards for individual-difference predictors in behavioral research \citep{cohen-1988-power}, where such magnitudes are considered ``small but real'' effects that compound at the aggregate level \citep{lakens-2013-effect-size}; thresholds for ``practically meaningful'' may differ in LLM systems where aggregation amplifies small per-instance biases.

\paragraph{Embedding anisotropy.}
Our centrality confound (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}) connects to representation degeneration: embeddings collapse into narrow cones \cite{gao-repr-degen-2019,ethayarajh-contextual-2019}, but local clusters can be isotropic \cite{cai-isotropy-clusters-2021}, explaining why MAVEN's single-ontology types conflate confusability with centrality.

\paragraph{SCS as a lightweight proxy.}
\label{sec:scs-validation}
SCS measures embedding-space similarity rather than task-grounded confusion; it is thus a pre-hoc proxy computable without running extraction experiments. Empirical validation confirms the proxy is informative: pairwise SCS correlates with observed type confusion on both NER and RE datasets (Few-NERD $r = 0.112$, FewRel $r = 0.187$, both $p < 0.001$; Table~\ref{tab:pairwise}). However, SCS is a partial proxy; context, type frequency, mention ambiguity, and definition quality also contribute to confusion (\S\ref{sec:pairwise}). Task-grounded confusion scores derived from empirical error matrices would capture these factors but require running extraction first, forfeiting the predictive utility that makes SCS useful for schema design.

